WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.620
Hey everyone, welcome to Models and Agents, episode 113 on July 16th, 2026.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.500
Let's see what shipped, another day, another round of AI developments.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:19.840
Let's break it down. OpenAI's GPT 5.6 sole cuts prompt injection failures by six times

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.080
through targeted red teaming, giving builders and noticeably safer base for agent loops.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:32.660
OpenAI reports that training against GPT red attacks produced a model six times more robust

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:38.660
than its predecessor from four months ago. The model was evaluated on previously unseen

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:45.400
attacks generated by GPT red and recorded six times fewer failures than the prior best

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:51.860
production model. This directly improves the reliability of any agent that accepts untrusted input

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:57.800
or tool output. Builders working on long running agents can now start from a checkpoint that

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:04.260
already incorporates this safety signal instead of adding post-hawk filters. Watch for follow-up

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:09.980
releases that extend the same training loop to other frontier models. This continues the frontier

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:16.260
model safety arc tracked since last episode, where closed labs have been iterating red teaming

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:22.380
techniques to close the gap between capability and alignment. Anthropic released new simulation

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:28.960
results showing four additional misalignment patterns in autonomous agents. ThinkE opened sourced

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:38.920
a 975 billion parameter multimodal model under Apache 2.0. SpaceX AI released the full rust agent harness

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:47.740
behind its coating CLI. ThinkE released its first full LLM under open weights. A 975 billion

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:56.360
parameter multimodal model plus a smaller 276 billion parameter variant. Both carry Apache 2.0

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:02.780
licensing. The release immediately becomes the strongest openly available American model in its class.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:09.340
Builders can now run a competitive multimodal model without closed API dependency or restrictive

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:15.600
commercial terms. The open weight release narrows the documented performance gap between closed

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:22.000
frontier families and community accessible checkpoints. A one trillion parameter model trained with

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.920
zero reinforcement learning reached competitive results on seven math benchmarks after a two-phase

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:34.240
training run. The pipeline added clipped important sampling and mixed precision controls to keep

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:40.520
training stable at this scale. Emergent behaviors observed include self-verification and parallel

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:47.140
reasoning traces. Team scaling reinforcement learning beyond 100 billion parameters should examine

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:53.480
the reported training inference ratio correction. The work directly tests the open question of whether

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:59.620
reasoning gains from test time compute continue to scale linearly once models exceed several hundred

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:06.320
billion parameters. Phi-4 was the strongest small model for Bangla health misinformation claim extraction

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.220
in the reported experiments. The authors paired it with a responsible natural language processing

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:19.220
framework that adds cultural sensitivity and harm potential checks. The work targets low resource

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:26.000
settings where large models remain impractical. Anyone building detection pipelines for non-English health

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.120
content can start with the Phi-4 baseline and the released framework. The Pattern SDK guide walks

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:39.020
through a complete voice agent workflow including dynamic caller variables, tool registration for

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:46.300
bookings, output guard rails, and a latency cost dashboard. The same logic maps to a Twilio plus

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:52.860
open AI real-time deployment. Developers can follow the scripted call flows and deterministic

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:59.520
eval harness to validate before going live. The post supplies the exact guard rail and eval patterns

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:06.640
needed for production phone agents. This update advances the agent tool use program by demonstrating

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.120
concrete guard rail patterns that address the reliability questions raised in recent episodes.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:20.240
SpaceX AI released the full Apache 2.0 rust code base covering the agent loop, tool dispatch,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:29.140
text user interface, and extension system used by its coding CLI. GROC 4.5 itself remains closed

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:35.580
and external contributions are not accepted. The release gives builders a ready to fork rust agent

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:42.260
skeleton for CLI style coding tools. Clone the repo if you need a high performance low-level

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:48.200
agent harness instead of Python frameworks. The open-sourced harness provides a concrete reference

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:53.680
implementation for the agent loop standardization questions that have been tracked since the last

00:04:53.680 --> 00:05:00.400
episode. Simon Willison extracted the rust mermaid rendering code and compiled it to Web Assembly

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:08.000
for direct browser use. The resulting tool runs entirely client side. Anyone embedding diagrams and

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.420
web apps can drop the Web Assembly module in place of server-side rendering. The no-act she who

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:22.020
claw framework adds a pluggable GUI sub agent with experience attributable memory and a self-evolving

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:29.800
skill library on top of open claw. It reports 64.1% on the long horizon mobile world benchmark

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:38.720
using open source kimi 2.6 models. Cross-platform support covers Android, iOS, Harmony OS, and Windows.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.720
The memory and skill components transfer across base models with an 8.5% lift.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:53.660
PCC-SQL applies a per token LUDGETS mask derived from column-use policies so that single query

00:05:53.660 --> 00:06:02.240
violations are deterministically eliminated during decoding. It achieves 0% leakage and up to 88.7%

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:08.620
coverage on spider a slash CU while staying within plus 10% tokens of direct prompting.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:14.540
The approach works across three open source models and three benchmarks. Teams that need

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:20.980
deterministic policy enforcement in text to SQL pipelines can adopt the grammar aligned masking

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:27.880
technique immediately. Evidence selection is cast as a QUBO problem that balances relevance,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:35.280
requirement coverage, redundancy, and compactness. The selector matches large language model-based set

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.560
selectors on hotpot QA, exact match, and token F1 while remaining solver compatible.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:48.540
This separates combinatorial selection from semantic generation, letting teams reserve large

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:55.160
language models for answer synthesis. PalmClaw runs the full agent loop, memory, skills, and

00:06:55.160 --> 00:07:00.440
tools directly on device and exposes mobile capabilities as explicit device tools.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:10.280
It records an 11.5% relative task success gain and 94.9% reduction in completion time versus the

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:17.560
strongest baseline. The design keeps every action inside clear execution boundaries. Mobile developers

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:23.540
looking for a native alternative to GUI automation agents can start from the open source release.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:29.900
Okay, let's pop the hood on cost-aware speculative decoding for mixture of experts models.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:35.660
Everyone talks about speculative decoding as a simple draft and verify speed up.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:42.480
In practice, the technique collides with the non-uniform memory cost of mixture of experts inference.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:49.680
When high probability draft tokens wrote to disjoint experts, the verification step triggers scattered

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:57.100
expert loads that erase most of the latency win. EcoSpec therefore adds a lightweight expert predictor

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:02.880
and a dynamic buffer that prefers draft pads whose experts are already resident in the current

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:10.440
verification set. The added predictor costs a few milliseconds, yet reclaims up to 1.62 times

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:18.340
end-to-end speed up on 671 billion scale mixture of experts models by cutting expert weight traffic.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:25.080
The key engineering decision is to keep the target model's verification rule unchanged so the method

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:31.880
works as a drop-in draft selector. Use it when your mixture of experts workload is memory bandwidth

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:39.540
bound and you already have a fast expert predictor. Skip it if expert activation is already dense or your

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:47.020
draft model is tiny enough that scattering is rare. Clone the SpaceX AI GROC build repo and run

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:53.180
its text user interface on a local coding task to see how the rust agent loop compares with Python

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:59.720
frameworks. Load thinky's inkling small weights for any multimodal retrieval workload you currently

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:06.920
route to a closed API. Add the client side GROC mermaid web assembly module to a dashboard that

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:14.280
renders diagrams without server-round trips. Run the pattern SDK restaurant booking example end-to-end

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:20.740
and compare its guardrail plus eval harness against your current voice agent stack. Expect further

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:27.860
open AI releases that apply the GPT red training loop to additional model families. Watch for more

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:33.880
open-weight multimodal releases now that thinky has set a new Apache 2.0 baseline.

